Firewall gateway collection in site backups
===========================================

When BackupService passes a site_id to the firewall contributor, gateway
connections must belong to that site AND the requested organization. The site
must not be soft-deleted. The SQL query excludes gateways from sibling sites,
organization-wide gateways with no site, deleted gateway rows, gateways owned
by another organization, and invalid cross-organization site associations.
Filtering happens before credential decryption and serialization.

Previously only firewall devices and their child rules/NAT/VPN tunnels honored
site_id; gateway connections from every site in the organization were included.
This could put unintended credentials in a passphrase-protected site backup.
The HTTP create/download routes already require an unscoped organization
administrator; this finding does not establish a lower-privilege HTTP bypass.

With no site_id, existing organization-wide gateway selection is unchanged,
including gateways without a site. Config snapshots still omit gateway
credentials and redact recognized secret settings. Vault snapshots still carry
decrypted credentials inside the encrypted archive. The firewall manifest's
secrets_excluded flag now correctly reflects the selected mode rather than
always claiming that secrets are absent.

This change is limited to the firewall contributor. Other contributors may
include organization-wide data in a site backup; this is NOT a guarantee that
the whole archive contains only data for one site. Core credentials, users and
their grants, automation, VPN records and camera grouping/view metadata require
separate scope review. Existing archives are not modified or reclassified.
Organization-wide lifecycle inconsistencies and authorization changes during
collection are not addressed here.

Qualification uses actual PostgreSQL rows and the real core/firewall collectors,
backup service, local file storage, compression, checksums and encryption. It
reads back and decrypts disposable config/vault files and checks their gateway
contents. This is not a hardware configuration capture, restore qualification,
remote-storage durability check, or HTTP authentication test. No database
migration, public schema, frontend or dependency change is required.
